diff --git a/docs/cli.md b/docs/cli.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..cd40884
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/cli.md
@@ -0,0 +1,73 @@
+# Command wiring
+
+The root command and migrated handlers use Kong v1.8.1. `main.go` constructs the
+command descriptions, builds the Kong parser, and runs the parsed context.
+Parsing failures, command errors, and invalid-invocation usage go to stderr and
+produce a non-zero exit status. Normal output and requested help go to stdout.
+Both `--help` and `arkade help COMMAND` render help through Kong.
+
+## Native commands
+
+`internal/cli.Command` describes a node in the Kong grammar. Its `Options` and
+`Persistent` fields point to Go structs with ordinary Kong flag tags, including
+`name`, `short`, `default`, and `hidden`. Handlers read those typed options;
+`cli.Changed` detects an explicit command-line flag, including an explicit false
+boolean, from the parsed Kong context rather than the flag's defaulted value.
+Kong type mappers retain the existing `strconv.ParseBool` boolean spellings and
+base-detected integer syntax, rather than changing accepted values to Kong's
+different built-in boolean and integer conventions.
+They also preserve shorthand `-f=value` forms and flag values beginning with a
+hyphen, including negative integers and repeatable string values.
+
+The grammar is assembled using Kong's plugin embedding support because the app
+catalog supplies commands dynamically. Flags bind to their original options
+structs, so handlers do not need to interpret arguments or perform flag parsing.
+Kong selects the command and calls the dispatcher through `Context.Run()`.
+Before parsing, variadic arguments are represented by optional scalar positional
+slots, sized from the argument count. This lets Kong parse flags interspersed
+with positional arguments, including multiple tool names or an OCI image and
+destination, without a second argument parser or an arbitrary positional limit.
+Help retains the compact variadic representation.
+
+Persistent flags live on the parent Kong node. Local flags on command groups
+live on an implicit, hidden default branch. This permits existing no-subcommand
+behavior without making local flags persistent. A Kong lifecycle hook rejects
+local flags when another branch is selected, including flags preceding that
+branch. Help uses Kong's help printer, with implicit branches omitted and their
+local flags shown on the parent command.
+
+Keep `get` before `docker` in the root registration order: both declare `d`, and
+the first matching alias must continue to select `get`.
+
+## Exempt installers and utilities
+
+`internal/legacy` is the boundary for the explicitly exempt Cobra leaf commands.
+It projects their existing flag declarations into the Kong grammar, including
+shorthands, defaults, hidden flags, required flags, and repeatable string arrays.
+After Kong parsing, it copies the parsed values into the leaf's existing flag
+storage and invokes its argument validation, pre-run, run, and post-run callbacks.
+It does not invoke Cobra's parser, command traversal, help, or completion runtime.
+
+Keep this adapter restricted to the exempt app installers and utility leaves.
+`cmd/apps_catalog.go` retains the existing app catalog; Argo CD is a native Kong
+handler, while the other app factories are adapted at that boundary. Installer
+implementations and the download, archive, Helm, and Kubernetes packages remain
+unchanged.
+
+## Completion
+
+`arkade completion bash|zsh|fish|powershell` emits scripts generated from the live
+Kong model. The scripts query the hidden `__complete` command for context-sensitive
+command names, aliases, subcommands, visible flags, and declared positional
+candidates. Completion does not construct or inspect a Cobra command tree.
+
+## Validation
+
+Run `go test ./...` for the existing tests and command-contract regression tests.
+The latter exercise all command and alias help paths, flag defaults and scope,
+catalog listings, error streams and exit status, completion, mocked release
+interactions, Argo CD's kubectl calls, and OCI credential storage. External
+release and Kubernetes operations use temporary executable fixtures, not live
+repositories or clusters.
+
+Dependencies are vendored; use `go test -mod=vendor ./...` to check that path too.
